 **Payload trade.** Full regenerative gNB: bent-pipe cannot serve users with no gateway in view, and split gNB (options 2/7.2x) keeps most RF/PHY power onboard while RRC still round-trips over the feeder.
 Power, thermal and radiation costs are mitigated by a narrowband gNB, duty-cycling over empty ocean and COTS processing under a rad-hard supervisor.
-The onboard gNB also sorts every message into one of three classes, which is why the scheduler must sit in orbit: critical (alarms, safety and shutdown commands, a few hundred bytes) takes the first uplink slot and the first authorised path on the current pass; regular (sensor telemetry, logs, files) is queued onboard and delivered by store-and-forward on a later pass (≤90 min); metadata (device status, position, delivery receipts and policy tags) is tiny but tells the twin and the policy engine where each asset is and which gateway it may use.
-A bent-pipe payload cannot make this call without a gateway in view.
